Arabs were unanimous in rejecting the Partition Plan, there was disagreement over the extent to which they should oppose it.

Hussein ibn Ali, emir of Transjordan, favored acquiescence in the plan, at least for the time being. Aidid al-Khalidi, prime minister of Greater Syria, advocated outright rejection. Ibn Saud's advisers divided into two factions, one favoring acceptance, the other refusal. On 12 August 1937, the Saudis issued a statement declaring that "the Government of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia rejects the plan which divides the country into two parts, and does not approve of it in any way whatsoever."^{1}

In an effort to forestall opposition to the plan, the British Foreign Office dispatched Anthony Eden to the Middle East for consultations with the Arab leaders. Eden arrived in Cairo on 20 August 1937, and spent the next two weeks shuttling between Amman, Damascus, Beirut, Haifa, Jaffa, Aqaba, and Jerusalem.

